[38;5;231m⏺[39m Done. Production stays verified; staging now trusts its own CA.

  [1m[38;5;153mclient.py[0m — added a [38;5;153mVERIFY[39m module constant read from [38;5;153mREPORTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m, falling back to [38;5;153mTrue[39m, and passed it as [38;5;153mverify=
[39m  on both [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls ([38;5;153mclient.py:11[39m, [38;5;153mclient.py:20[39m). Unset means the system trust store, so
  [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m is unchanged in behavior and still fully validates.

  [1m[38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[0m — sets [38;5;153mREPORTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m (via [38;5;153msetdefault[39m, so you can override) to [38;5;153mcerts/staging-ca.pem[39m
  resolved relative to the repo root.

  One thing you need to do: [1mthat cert file doesn't exist yet.[0m Export the staging CA and save it to [38;5;153mcerts/staging-ca.pem[39m:

  openssl s_client -showcerts -connect reports.staging.example:443 </dev/null 2>/dev/null \
    | openssl x509 -outform PEM > certs/staging-ca.pem

  (If staging's cert is signed by a private CA rather than being self-signed, use the CA's PEM, not the leaf.) Until
  that file is there, [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m fails immediately with a "Could not find a suitable TLS CA certificate bundle"
  error — loud rather than silently insecure.

  Checks: [38;5;153mruff format --check[39m clean, both files byte-compile. [38;5;153mruff check[39m reports two RUF100 "unused noqa" on the [38;5;153m# noqa:
[39m  [38;5;153mE402[39m import comments — pre-existing, fires in the untouched [38;5;153mexport_prod.py[39m too, and only because there's no project
  ruff config enabling E402; left alone. I could not make a live request to either host, so the handshake itself is
  unverified.

